Nested Classes in Java

-------------------------------

What are nested classes:
- a class defined inside another class
- used to logically group classes that belong together,
  improve encapsulation, and keep code closer to where it's used
- 4 types: static nested, inner (non-static), local, anonymous

-------------------------------

1. Static Nested Class:
- declared with static keyword inside an outer class
- belongs to the outer CLASS, not to any instance of it
- cannot access non-static members of the outer class directly
  (needs an object reference to do so, just like any other class)
- can be instantiated without creating an instance of the outer class

  example:
      class Outer {
          private static int x = 10;
          private int y = 20;

          static class Nested {
              void show() {
                  System.out.println(x);   // allowed — static member
                  System.out.println(y);   // compile-time error — non-static member
              }
          }
      }
      Outer.Nested obj = new Outer.Nested(); // no Outer instance needed

  when to use: when the nested class doesn't need access to instance members
               of the outer class (logically grouped but independent)

-------------------------------

2. Inner Class (Non-Static Nested Class):
- declared without static keyword inside an outer class
- tied to an instance of the outer class — cannot exist without one
- has direct access to ALL members of the outer class, including private ones

  example:
      class Outer {
          private int x = 10;

          class Inner {
              void show() {
                  System.out.println(x); // allowed — has access to outer instance
              }
          }
      }
      Outer outer = new Outer();
      Outer.Inner inner = outer.new Inner(); // outer instance required

  when to use: when the inner class needs access to instance fields/methods
               of the outer class

-------------------------------

3. Local Class:
- defined inside a method or a block, not at class level
- only visible within that method/block — cannot be used outside it
- can access members of the enclosing class and local variables
  that are final or effectively final (not reassigned after declaration)
- cannot have access modifiers (private/public/etc.)

  example:
      class Outer {
          void doSomething() {
              final String message = "Hello";

              class Local {
                  void print() {
                      System.out.println(message); // allowed — effectively final
                  }
              }
              new Local().print();
          }
      }

  when to use: when you need a class with multiple methods used only
               in one specific method — rare in practice

-------------------------------

4. Anonymous Class:
- a class with no name, defined and instantiated at the same time
- used to override a class or implement an interface on the spot, once
- cannot have a constructor (no name to call)
- can access enclosing class members and effectively final local variables

  example:
      Runnable r = new Runnable() {
          public void run() {
              System.out.println("Running!");
          }
      };
      r.run();

  when to use: when you need a one-time implementation of an interface
               or subclass, without creating a named class for it
  note: since Java 8, lambda expressions replaced most anonymous class
        use cases when only one method needs to be implemented

-------------------------------

Quick reference:
- Static Nested — no outer instance needed, accesses static members only
- Inner — requires outer instance, accesses all outer members (even private)
- Local — exists only inside a method, accesses effectively final locals
- Anonymous — no name, defined and used once, no constructor allowed